    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "These files includes movie files of reinjection experiments. The drops were stabilized by nanoparticles. \r\n\r\nParameters:\r\n - The drop size and channel geometry in the movies were all the same. \r\n - The diameter of drops was 68 um. \r\n - The channel width and height were 38 and 30 um respectively. \r\n - The percentage of glycerol in water in disperse phase was 20 and 40%.\r\n - The size of nanoparticles were 30, 100, and 200 nm.\r\n - The concentration of NPs was 0 (wash out), 3, and 6% in HFE (w/w)",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Gai, Y, Kim, M, Pan, M, and Tang, SKY. (2017). Amphiphilic nanoparticles suppress droplet break-up in a concentrated emulsion flowing through a narrow constriction. Biomicrofluidics. 11:034117. https://doi.org//10.1063/1.4985158",
